.TH PAGEDAEMON 7 "2026-05-30" "Substrate" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
pagedaemon \- kernel page-replacement (pageout) daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pagedaemon
.RI ( kernel\ process )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pagedaemon
is a kernel process that keeps the supply of free physical pages above a
set of watermarks by reclaiming pages that are not in active use.  It is the
agent of Substrate's demand-paged virtual memory: when memory runs low it
ages pages out of the active working set, writes dirty pages to backing
store, and frees clean pages back to the physical allocator.
.PP
The daemon runs as a dedicated kernel thread with no user address space.  It
appears in the process table with the command name
.B pagedaemon
and is flagged as a kernel task; its PID is assigned dynamically at boot.  It
runs in the time-sharing scheduling class at a low priority (just above the
idle class), so it yields readily to user and interactive work and only
consumes CPU when reclamation is actually needed.
.PP
The daemon is spawned once, late in boot, from
.B vm_page_late_init()
(see
.BR FILES ).
Until it has been spawned, reclamation requests are serviced from the idle
loop of the swapper (PID 0) instead, so the system can make forward progress
under memory pressure before the daemon exists.
.SS Operation
The daemon sleeps until it is needed and wakes on either of two events:
an explicit wakeup when the free-page count falls toward the low watermark,
or a periodic timeout (roughly once per second).  On each wake cycle it:
.IP \(bu 3
optionally ages pages when the approximate-LRU replacement policy is active;
.IP \(bu 3
runs the pageout pass if a shortage is signalled or if its own heuristic
.RB ( vm_page_should_pageout() )
judges that the free pool is below target or that too many dirty pages have
accumulated;
.IP \(bu 3
voluntarily yields the CPU.
.PP
The pageout pass itself proceeds in stages, escalating only as far as is
needed to reach the free-page target:
.IP "1. Cache drain" 4
The slab/UMA caches are asked to return unused backing pages
.RB ( uma_reclaim() ).
.IP "2. Reclaim clean inactive pages" 4
Pages on the inactive queue that are not dirty are freed immediately.
.IP "3. Launder dirty inactive pages" 4
Dirty inactive pages are moved to the laundry queue and written back through
their object's pager (the swap pager for anonymous memory, the vnode pager
for file-backed memory).  A page that writes back successfully is marked
clean and becomes reclaimable; a page whose writeback fails is reactivated so
the daemon does not spin on it.
.IP "4. Age active pages" 4
The active queue is scanned and unreferenced pages are demoted to the
inactive queue, replenishing the pool of eviction candidates.  Under the
default CLOCK policy the scan consults each page's hardware reference bit
(via the pmap layer) and gives referenced pages a second chance; under the
approximate-LRU policy it maintains a small per-page age counter instead.
.PP
If the system is critically short of memory and reclamation cannot meet the
target, the pageout path invokes an out-of-memory victim selection as a last
resort.
.SS Page queues
Every managed physical page sits on exactly one queue:
.TP
.B active
Recently referenced pages forming the working set.
.TP
.B inactive
Aged pages that are the primary eviction candidates.
.TP
.B laundry
A transient queue holding dirty pages while their writeback is in flight.
.TP
.B wired
Pinned pages (kernel memory, DMA buffers, anything with a non-zero wire
count) that are exempt from paging.
.PP
Free pages are not queued here; they are returned to the buddy physical
allocator (see
.BR HISTORY ).
The daemon drives pages between queues by manipulating per-page state flags
including the active, inactive, dirty, writeback-in-progress, and busy
indicators.
.SS Watermarks
Reclamation is governed by free-page watermarks, expressed in pages.  Their
boot defaults are scaled up at startup in proportion to installed RAM
.RB ( vm_page_tune_thresholds() ),
taking the larger of the default and the RAM-derived value:
.TP
.B free_reserved
Pages held in reserve for kernel emergency allocations (default 8).
.TP
.B free_min
The critical floor; dropping below it is treated as a hard shortage
(default 16).
.TP
.B free_target
The level the daemon reclaims up to before going back to sleep
(default 64).
.TP
.B inactive_target
The desired size of the inactive queue, i.e. how many eviction candidates to
keep staged (default 128).
.SS Backing store
Anonymous (non-file-backed) pages are laundered to a swap area through the
swap pager.  Swap is activated against a file or block device and tracked
with a bitmap-based block allocator.  File-backed pages are written back
through the vnode pager to their originating file.  A page that has been
written to swap is tagged accordingly so a later fault can read it back in.
.SS Tuning and control
The replacement policy can be switched between CLOCK (the default) and
approximate-LRU at run time
.RB ( vm_page_set_policy() ).
The daemon can also be suspended, in which case it wakes, observes the
suspend flag, and immediately yields without reclaiming \(em useful for
debugging memory behavior.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /proc/vmstat
Live VM counters maintained as the daemon and fault handler run: free,
active, inactive, wired and laundry page counts, plus cumulative pageins,
pageouts, faults, copy-on-write faults, reactivations and zero-filled pages.
.TP
.B /proc/meminfo
Summary of total, free and used physical memory.
.TP
.B /proc/memtrack
Per-call-site physical-page allocation accounting.
.TP
.B sys/vm/vm_page.c
Daemon entry point
.RB ( vm_pagedaemon() ),
the pageout pass
.RB ( vm_pageout() ),
queue management and the watermark definitions.
.TP
.B sys/vm/vm_swap.c
Swap pager and swap-area management.
.TP
.B sys/vm/vm_pager.c
Pager abstraction shared by the swap, vnode and device pagers.
.SH NOTES
The page laundering step performs its writeback synchronously while the
daemon holds the page, so a slow or wedged backing store can stall
reclamation; this is mitigated by reactivating pages whose writeback fails.
Because the daemon runs below interactive priority, sustained heavy paging is
visible as the daemon competing for CPU only when free memory is genuinely
scarce.
.SH HISTORY
The page daemon is part of Substrate's BSD-style virtual memory subsystem,
which layers a vm_object/pager model and active/inactive page queues over a
buddy physical-memory allocator and a UMA-backed kernel heap.
